# The ledger of `term`: what a program can do with its terminal, one block per
# capability, and what Rebel answers. The lists are this machine's,
# 2026-11-16: Python 3.15's `getpass`, `tty`, `termios`, `curses` (296 public
# names, read by `dir()`; its constants are grouped below by what they are) and
# `shutil.get_terminal_size`; Go 1.27's `golang.org/x/term`, which this
# machine holds vendored in the toolchain (`go doc golang.org/x/term`); Rust's
# standard library's `std::io::IsTerminal`. The author's request named
# Rust's `crossterm` and `indicatif` and Python's `rich`, none of which is on
# this machine: their lines are their public items as their documentation
# names them, read as capabilities, and no source of theirs was opened.
# Added 2026-11-17 (the line editor and the tables, the author's decision of
# that day after a census of Odin, Zig, Java and .NET): Python 3.15's
# `readline` (28 public names, read by `dir()`) and `cmd.Cmd` (30), and Go
# 1.27's `text/tabwriter` (`go doc -all text/tabwriter`).

capability term/is-terminal  whether a stream is a terminal
  heroes  io.is_terminal
  go      term.IsTerminal
  rust    std::io::IsTerminal, crossterm::tty::IsTty
  python  os.isatty, io.IOBase.isatty
  note    `io`'s, which every stream has; `term.colors_on` asks it with the environment's words besides

capability term/size  the terminal's columns and rows
  heroes  os.terminal_size
  go      term.GetSize, term.Terminal.SetSize
  rust    crossterm::terminal::size
  python  shutil.get_terminal_size, os.get_terminal_size, termios.tcgetwinsize, tty.tcgetwinsize, curses.update_lines_cols, curses.is_term_resized
  note    `os`'s, read from the system; setting the size is a terminal emulator's act, below

capability term/set-size  the terminal's size set by the program
  heroes  refused: the size is the window's, which the person sets; a program that sets a pseudo-terminal's size is a terminal emulator, out of this area
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::terminal::SetSize
  python  termios.tcsetwinsize, tty.tcsetwinsize, curses.resizeterm, curses.resize_term

capability term/colors  the sixteen colours of text and of its background
  heroes  term.Color, term.with_foreground, term.with_background, term.styled
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::style::Color, crossterm::style::SetForegroundColor, crossterm::style::SetBackgroundColor, crossterm::style::ResetColor, crossterm::style::Stylize
  python  rich.style.Style, rich.color.Color, curses.start_color, curses.has_colors, curses.init_pair, curses.color_pair, curses.pair_content, curses.pair_number, curses.color_content, curses.use_default_colors, curses.assume_default_colors, curses.COLOR_BLACK and the seven other COLOR_ constants
  note    the sequences are ECMA-48's (SGR 30 to 37 and 40 to 47) and the bright ones xterm's (90 to 97, 100 to 107), as crossterm writes them; asserted as those standards write them, no library being on this machine to ask

capability term/colors-256  the 256 colours of the palette
  heroes  term.Color
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::style::Color::AnsiValue
  python  rich.color.Color (color(n)), curses.COLORS, curses.has_extended_color_support
  note    `.indexed(n: u8)`: the type holds 0 to 255, so no index is out of range

capability term/true-color  a colour of 24 bits
  heroes  term.Color
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::style::Color::Rgb
  python  rich.color.Color.from_rgb, rich.color.Color.parse ("#rrggbb")
  note    `.rgb(red:, green:, blue:)`, each a `u8`; a terminal of fewer colours shows its nearest, as it does for every program

capability term/palette-redefinition  a colour of the terminal's palette changed
  heroes  refused: it changes the person's terminal past the program's end, and what it was before cannot be read back on every terminal
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  curses.init_color, curses.can_change_color

capability term/attributes  bold, dim, italic, underline, blink, reverse, hidden, strike
  heroes  term.Attribute, term.with_attribute, term.Style, term.plain
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::style::Attribute, crossterm::style::SetAttribute, crossterm::style::SetAttributes, crossterm::style::Attributes
  python  rich.style.Style (bold, dim, italic, underline, blink, reverse, conceal, strike), curses.A_BOLD, curses.A_DIM, curses.A_ITALIC, curses.A_UNDERLINE, curses.A_BLINK, curses.A_REVERSE, curses.A_INVIS, curses.A_STANDOUT, curses.termattrs
  note    the eight every terminal of ECMA-48 takes; crossterm's double underline, framed, encircled and overlined are left out, most terminals drawing none of them

capability term/no-color  colour written only where it shows, and plain text elsewhere
  heroes  term.colors_on, term.write_styled
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::style::force_color_output, crossterm's own NO_COLOR reading
  python  rich.console.Console (no_color, force_terminal, color_system)
  note    no colour where the stream is no terminal, `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty (no-color.org's words), or `TERM` is `dumb`; a program that wants colour in a file writes `styled` itself

capability term/windows-sequences  the Windows console made to take the sequences
  heroes  term.colors_on, term.write_control
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::ansi_support::supports_ansi
  python  rich.console.Console (legacy_windows)
  note    the console's processing of sequences is turned on where it is off and put back when the program ends; a console too old to take them is told as no terminal, and gets plain text

capability term/cursor-move  the cursor put at a cell or moved by a count
  heroes  term.cursor_to, term.cursor_up, term.cursor_down, term.cursor_forward, term.cursor_back
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::cursor::MoveTo, crossterm::cursor::MoveUp, crossterm::cursor::MoveDown, crossterm::cursor::MoveLeft, crossterm::cursor::MoveRight, crossterm::cursor::MoveToColumn, crossterm::cursor::MoveToRow, crossterm::cursor::MoveToNextLine, crossterm::cursor::MoveToPreviousLine
  python  rich.control.Control.move_to, curses.setsyx, curses.getsyx
  note    rows and columns from 1, as the terminal counts them; crossterm counts from 0. A column alone or a row alone is `cursor_to` with the other known

capability term/cursor-position  where the cursor stands, read back from the terminal
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::cursor::position
  python  curses.getsyx
  note    it asks the terminal (`ESC [ 6 n`) and reads its answer in the raw mode, between keys the person may type: a reader that sets those keys aside for `read_key` is the work, not yet done

capability term/cursor-visibility  the cursor hidden and shown
  heroes  term.cursor_hide, term.cursor_show
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::cursor::Hide, crossterm::cursor::Show, crossterm::cursor::EnableBlinking, crossterm::cursor::DisableBlinking, crossterm::cursor::SetCursorStyle
  python  curses.curs_set, rich.console.Console.show_cursor
  note    blinking and the cursor's shape are left out, many terminals keeping their own

capability term/cursor-save  the cursor's place saved and brought back
  heroes  term.cursor_save, term.cursor_restore
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::cursor::SavePosition, crossterm::cursor::RestorePosition
  python  -
  note    DEC's `ESC 7` and `ESC 8`, which every terminal of the VT100's line takes

capability term/clear  a line or the screen cleared
  heroes  term.clear_line, term.clear_to_line_end, term.clear_screen
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::terminal::Clear, crossterm::terminal::ClearType
  python  rich.console.Console.clear, curses.erasechar
  note    ClearType's FromCursorDown, FromCursorUp and Purge are left out: a program that draws from the top clears the screen, and Purge (the scrollback) is the person's

capability term/control-written  a control sequence written only to a terminal
  heroes  term.write_control
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::execute, crossterm::queue
  python  rich.console.Console.control
  note    so a program moving the cursor writes nothing stray into a file

capability term/bell  the terminal's bell
  heroes  term.bell
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  curses.beep, curses.flash

capability term/alternate-screen  a screen of the program's own, the person's put back after
  heroes  pending
  go      -
