capability term/scroll  the screen's lines scrolled
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::terminal::ScrollUp, crossterm::terminal::ScrollDown
  python  -

capability term/title  the window's title set
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::terminal::SetTitle
  python  rich.console.Console.set_window_title

capability term/raw-mode  each key as it is pressed, nothing echoed, no key ending the program
  heroes  term.enter_raw, term.leave_raw
  go      term.MakeRaw, term.Restore, term.GetState, term.State
  rust    crossterm::terminal::enable_raw_mode, crossterm::terminal::disable_raw_mode, crossterm::terminal::is_raw_mode_enabled
  python  tty.setraw, tty.setcbreak, tty.cfmakeraw, tty.cfmakecbreak, termios.tcgetattr, termios.tcsetattr, curses.raw, curses.noraw, curses.cbreak, curses.nocbreak, curses.echo, curses.noecho, curses.nl, curses.nonl, curses.intrflush, curses.meta, curses.savetty, curses.resetty, curses.def_prog_mode, curses.def_shell_mode, curses.reset_prog_mode, curses.reset_shell_mode
  note    one mode, raw as x/term and crossterm make it, the output's newline kept so a program's lines still return to the left; the cbreak mode (signals kept) is left out, Ctrl-C reaching the program as a key instead, so a terminal is never left raw by a Ctrl-C the program did not see. Measured in a pseudo-terminal on this Mac (2026-11-16, `rebel-scratch/term-probe`): ECHO, ICANON and ISIG off in the mode, all three back after `leave_raw`, after `exit(3)` with no leave, and after a SIGTERM that still ended the program with signal 15

capability term/restored-at-exit  the terminal put back when the program ends without putting it back
  heroes  term.enter_raw
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  curses.wrapper
  note    `main` returning, `exit`, and on POSIX SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGQUIT where the program left them to their default (measured, above); a panic, a memory fault and SIGKILL cannot be reached by any library, the runtime setting SIGABRT's default before it aborts, and the face says so. Go, crossterm and Python's tty leave it to the program's own defer or finally

capability term/read-key  one key read as it is pressed, named
  heroes  term.read_key, term.Key
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::event::read, crossterm::event::Event::Key, crossterm::event::KeyEvent, crossterm::event::KeyCode, crossterm::event::KeyEventKind
  python  curses.getch, curses.getkey, curses.get_wch, curses.keyname, curses.unctrl, curses.has_key, curses.KEY_UP and the other KEY_ constants, msvcrt.getwch (Windows)
  note    the keys of xterm's sequences, which Linux's console, macOS's Terminal and Windows' console send, and which the layer writes for a Windows key with no character; a key typed ahead is the next one read, never lost (measured: `xy` typed at once read as `x` then `y`). Escape alone is told after 50 ms with nothing after it, crossterm's and curses' way (curses.get_escdelay, curses.set_escdelay)

capability term/read-key-timeout  a key waited for at most a given time
  heroes  term.read_key_within
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::event::poll
  python  curses.halfdelay, curses.napms, curses.typeahead
  note    in milliseconds, `net`'s unit for its deadlines: `time` is not in this package's row. Repaired 2026-11-19: on macOS a limited read never timed out and an Escape alone waited for the next key, since Darwin's poll(2) answers POLLNVAL for `/dev/tty` ("does not support devices", its BUGS) and the layer then waited in a read; the layer's wait is now poll, and select(2) where poll answers POLLNVAL, a descriptor at or past FD_SETSIZE waited on through a copy below it and `failed` (EMFILE) where every one below is taken. Measured in a pseudo-terminal (`rebel-scratch/repairs4/term`): with no key sent, `read_key_within(200)` ended `timeout` after 200 to 210 ms on this Mac, where it hung until killed at 6 s before; Escape alone was told 53 and 59 ms after it was sent, where it waited 605 and 200 ms for the next key; with 1,100 files open first, `failed` at once with every descriptor below 1,024 taken and `timeout` after 205 ms with one freed. Linux arm64 answered alike before and after (200 ms; 51 and 52 ms). The floor's tests run in a pseudo-terminal hung at their first wait before the repair and pass after, a 200 ms limit among them

capability term/key-modifiers  Ctrl, Alt and Shift told beside any key
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    crossterm::event::KeyModifiers, crossterm::event::KeyboardEnhancementFlags, crossterm::event::PushKeyboardEnhancementFlags
  python  curses.KEY_SLEFT and the other shifted KEY_ constants
  note    Ctrl with a letter is `Key.control`; a modified arrow (`ESC [ 1 ; 5 A`) is `Key.other` with its bytes, until its parameters are read into a key of their own

capability term/mouse-paste-focus-resize  the mouse, a paste, the focus and the window's resizing as events
  heroes  pending
  go      term.Terminal.SetBracketedPasteMode, term.ErrPasteIndicator
  rust    crossterm::event::EnableMouseCapture, crossterm::event::DisableMouseCapture, crossterm::event::MouseEvent, crossterm::event::EnableBracketedPaste, crossterm::event::Event::Paste, crossterm::event::EnableFocusChange, crossterm::event::Event::FocusGained, crossterm::event::Event::Resize
  python  curses.mousemask, curses.getmouse, curses.ungetmouse, curses.mouseinterval, curses.KEY_RESIZE

capability term/read-secret  a line read with nothing echoed, as a password
  heroes  term.read_secret
  go      term.ReadPassword, term.Terminal.ReadPassword
  rust    -
  python  getpass.getpass, getpass.GetPassWarning, getpass.unix_getpass, getpass.win_getpass, getpass.fallback_getpass
  note    read from the program's terminal and its prompt written there, as getpass does on POSIX, so a program whose input is a pipe still asks the person; Ctrl-C fails `stopped` and Ctrl-D on an empty line `closed`, where getpass raises; with no terminal it fails `unsupported`, where getpass falls back to reading the input with a warning, which would echo a password

capability term/user-name  the name of the user, beside getpass
  heroes  os.user_name
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  getpass.getuser
  note    `os`'s

capability term/line-editor  a line read with editing: the cursor moved by a character, a word or to an end, characters taken out, text killed and yanked
  heroes  term.read_line, term.press, term.line_editor, term.Editor, term.Pressed, term.Ending
  go      term.NewTerminal, term.Terminal, term.Terminal.ReadLine, term.Terminal.SetPrompt
  rust    -
  python  input (with readline loaded), readline.get_line_buffer, readline.redisplay, readline.backend, rich.prompt.Prompt
  note    Emacs's keys as GNU Readline and x/term bind them: Ctrl-A, E, B, F, K, U, W, Y, D, L, P, N, Alt-B, Alt-F, Alt-D, Alt-Backspace, Ctrl and Alt with the arrows; kills in a row add up, as Readline's do. Alt comes as Escape and the key, Readline's Meta prefix, the layer handing Escape alone. `press` is the machine, a key in and the bytes to draw out, so every key is tested with no terminal; driven in a pseudo-terminal on this Mac (2026-11-17, `rebel-scratch/term-probe/small`): words, a kill and its yank, a completion listed, the history, Ctrl-C and Ctrl-D, the terminal put back after. The line is drawn on one row, a window of it around the cursor where it is wider (linenoise's way), and the cursor put back by DEC's `ESC 7`/`ESC 8` so the terminal's own widths place it; Readline's lines that wrap over several rows are not drawn. An input that is no terminal is read as it stands with no prompt, as Python's own prompt and rustyline do, where Python's `input` writes its prompt; a letter and its accents move and go as one, where Readline takes one code point

capability term/line-history  the lines entered kept, browsed with Up and Down, and saved to a file and read back
  heroes  term.Editor, term.save_history, term.load_history
  go      term.History, term.Terminal.History
  rust    -
  python  readline.add_history, readline.clear_history, readline.get_current_history_length, readline.get_history_item, readline.get_history_length, readline.set_history_length, readline.remove_history_item, readline.replace_history_item, readline.read_history_file, readline.write_history_file, readline.set_auto_history
  note    the history is the editor's list, `history`, the oldest first: adding, clearing, reading, removing and replacing an entry are the list's built-ins, and its bound is `history_most`, 1,000 at first where Readline keeps all and x/term 100. A line entered is added as Readline's auto-history adds it, but an empty one or one equal to the newest (bash's `ignoredups`, x/term adding every one); `history_most` 0 keeps none, Python's `set_auto_history(False)`. The file is a line each, as Python's; a line holding a line break fails `invalid` rather than be split

capability term/line-completion  the word before the cursor completed by the program at Tab
  heroes  term.press_completing, term.read_line_completing, term.Completions, term.complete_words
  go      term.Terminal.AutoCompleteCallback
  rust    -
  python  readline.set_completer, readline.get_completer, readline.get_begidx, readline.get_endidx, readline.set_completer_delims, readline.get_completer_delims, readline.get_completion_type, readline.set_completion_display_matches_hook, cmd.Cmd.complete, cmd.Cmd.completenames, cmd.Cmd.completedefault, cmd.Cmd.complete_help
  note    no closure: the program's function takes its own state by value, the line and the cursor, and answers where the replaced text begins (Readline's begidx, the cursor its endidx) and the candidates, so its delimiters are its own; one candidate replaces, several put their common start, a first Tab with nothing to add rings and a second lists them in columns, as Readline does. A candidate is put as it is, where Readline adds a space after a single one; `complete_words` is `cmd.Cmd.completenames` over a list

capability term/line-search  the history searched as letters are typed, Readline's Ctrl-R
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  - (a binding of readline, no entry of the module)
  note    an incremental search draws its own prompt over the line and needs its own state in the editor; not written, x/term having none

capability term/line-prefilled  a line read with text already in it
  heroes  pending
